<header class="nx-ihero"><div class="wrap"><p class="nx-meta"><span>Twinthos Guides</span><span>Updated 13 October 2026</span></p><h1>The numbers behind the work.</h1><p class="nx-dek">Short, sourced guides on where time goes, what AI is actually doing to jobs, and what history says about work that changes shape.</p><ul class="nx-topics"><li><span>Admin time</span></li><li><span>AI adoption</span></li><li><span>Jobs</span></li><li><span>History of work</span></li></ul></div></header>
<div class="wrap"><a class="nx-lead" href="/notes/hours-lost-to-admin/"><span class="imgbox"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="/assets/notes-img/night-desk-800.webp 800w, /assets/notes-img/night-desk-1600.webp 1600w" sizes="(max-width:900px) 100vw, 1160px"><img src="/assets/notes-img/night-desk-1600.jpg" srcset="/assets/notes-img/night-desk-800.jpg 800w, /assets/notes-img/night-desk-1600.jpg 1600w" sizes="(max-width:900px) 100vw, 1160px" width="1600" height="900" alt="A single bare desk lit by one lamp in an empty office at night, a window with blurred city lights behind it" fetchpriority="high"></picture></span><span><span class="nx-tag">Latest &middot; Admin time</span><h2>Owners lose 11&nbsp;hours a week to admin. Here is the bill.</h2><p>Six working days a month on admin, 3.6 on sales. The arithmetic, with a calculator for your own week.</p><span class="nx-card-m">6 min read &middot; 8 October 2026</span><span class="more">Read the guide &rarr;</span></span></a></div>
<div class="wrap nx-igrid"><div class="nx-cards"><a class="nx-card" href="/notes/ai-adoption-gap-2026/"><span class="imgbox"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="/assets/notes-img/office-lit-windows-800.webp" sizes="(max-width:700px) 100vw, 380px"><img src="/assets/notes-img/office-lit-windows-800.jpg" width="800" height="450" alt="A glass office building at dusk, a grid of windows almost all dark with a few lit" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></picture></span><span class="nx-card-b"><span class="nx-tag">AI adoption</span><span class="nx-card-t">88&nbsp;percent of organisations use AI. Almost none run agents at scale.</span><span class="nx-card-d">Stanford's 2026 AI Index measures the gap between trying AI and running work through it.</span><span class="nx-card-m">7 min read &middot; 8 October 2026</span></span></a><a class="nx-card" href="/notes/entry-level-jobs-ai-2026/"><span class="imgbox"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="/assets/notes-img/ladder-800.webp" sizes="(max-width:700px) 100vw, 380px"><img src="/assets/notes-img/ladder-800.jpg" width="800" height="450" alt="A tall plain wooden ladder leaning against a bare concrete wall, the lowest rungs in window light and the upper rungs in shadow" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></picture></span><span class="nx-card-b"><span class="nx-tag">Jobs</span><span class="nx-card-t">AI is hitting the first rung of the career ladder first</span><span class="nx-card-d">Stanford's latest data: young workers in AI-exposed jobs are 19% below trend, and the cause is fewer hires, not layoffs.</span><span class="nx-card-m">7 min read &middot; 8 October 2026</span></span></a><a class="nx-card" href="/notes/wef-jobs-2030/"><span class="imgbox"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="/assets/notes-img/rails-800.webp" sizes="(max-width:700px) 100vw, 380px"><img src="/assets/notes-img/rails-800.jpg" width="800" height="450" alt="A railway track splitting into two lines at points, running across an empty flat landscape under a wide overcast sky" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></picture></span><span class="nx-card-b"><span class="nx-tag">Jobs</span><span class="nx-card-t">170&nbsp;million jobs created, 92&nbsp;million displaced by 2030</span><span class="nx-card-d">The WEF's Future of Jobs Report 2025: a net gain of 78&nbsp;million jobs, and 11 in every 100 workers who need training but are unlikely to get it.</span><span class="nx-card-m">6 min read &middot; 8 October 2026</span></span></a><a class="nx-card" href="/notes/horse-population-1915-1960/"><span class="imgbox"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="/assets/notes-img/horse-stable-800.webp" sizes="(max-width:700px) 100vw, 380px"><img src="/assets/notes-img/horse-stable-800.jpg" width="800" height="450" alt="An empty wooden horse stall in a dim stable, a single shaft of light on the straw and a tractor wheel visible beyond the open door" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></picture></span><span class="nx-card-b"><span class="nx-tag">History</span><span class="nx-card-t">The horse did not lose to a better horse. It lost to a different job.</span><span class="nx-card-d">26.7&nbsp;million farm horses and mules in 1918, about 3&nbsp;million in 1960. What the USDA and NBER numbers say about how a technology shift really unfolds.</span><span class="nx-card-m">7 min read &middot; 8 October 2026</span></span></a></div></div>
<div class="wrap"><div class="nx-method"><div><b>Primary sources</b><p>Figures come from the publisher of the data: government statistics, Stanford, the World Economic Forum. Not from second-hand summaries.</p></div><div><b>Every claim linked</b><p>Numbered citations in the text point to a source list at the bottom of each guide.</p></div><div><b>Corrections logged</b><p>When we change a number, the change is written at the foot of the guide with the date.</p></div></div></div>
<section class="nx-end"><div class="wrap nx-end-in"><div><h2>Want your own number, not an average?</h2><p>The operations audit measures where your team&rsquo;s hours go and what each repeating task costs, then ranks what to hand off first.</p></div><div class="nx-end-act"><a class="nx-btn" href="/operations-audit/">See the operations audit</a><a class="nx-btn ghost" href="/pricing/">Pricing</a></div></div></section>
</main>
